!
! NOTES
! - e, Vac and Vrf are charge-related energies; all the others are spin-related. The former can be scaled, but must be clearly larger than the spin energies.
! - More Pade terms -> slower time evolution.
! - Run the steady state first and check convergence: the up and down occupations must be equal (half and half, summing to 1). Check that the output contains "rho is found OK!"; use grep relative to check the quality of the Pade expansion (the smaller the better).
! - The steady-state run usually takes a few hours. Check the evolution of the voltage.
! - During the dynamics run the current is written continuously; check that it does not differ by orders of magnitude from the steady state, to make sure the data have not diverged (a large time step can easily make it blow up).
! - The oscillation frequency corresponds to the ESR frequency, and the time-evolution frequency to the Rabi frequency. Plot after the signal becomes stationary, then average over time to obtain one point; combining points at different frequencies gives the simulated ESR map.
!
! OUTPUT
! - Steady state (output), the line starting with IVOUTPUT: left/right electrode voltages, up/down spin occupations, left/right electrode currents.
! - Dynamics (output), e.g.:
!     19999.9900  -0.501399E+000  0.648586E+000  0.498472E+000  0.497779E+000  0.190610E+000
!     -> time, left/right electrode currents, up/down spin occupations.
! - Dynamics (curr.data): col 1 = time; col 2/3 = left/right electrode voltages; col 4/5 = left/right electrode currents; col 6 = left+right current; col 7/8 = up/down spin occupations; col 9 = up+down occupations.
!
! POST-PROCESSING
! - Set the sweep frequency, run the calculation, then average the local minima of the current (column 5 of curr.data) over the last part of the time evolution to obtain the STM-ESR current at that frequency.
! - Scanning over frequencies around the resonance gives the simulated STM-ESR map.
